  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 212 AM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: We await the arrival of showers this morning as a closed \nupper low slowly pivots eastward across the central/eastern Great \nLakes. Scattered to numerous showers can already be seen out ahead \nof this feature. This area of showers will continue to slowly drift \neast-northeastward as its parent low crosses into southern NY, \neventually pivoting into our southern sections along a weak inverted \nsurface trough. This band of showers will rotate over NY through \nthis afternoon before shifting east into VT late today and overnight \nas the upper low spins across southern New England and eventually \noffshore by Sunday morning. Hence expect fairly widespread showers \nthrough the day and into tonight, then becoming more focused in the \nnorthern mountains Sunday morning as west-northwest flow develops \nbehind the departing low pressure. Snow levels drop to 2200-3200 ft \nas the cold pool moves overhead, and expect some slushy snow \naccumulation will be possible tonight into Sunday morning, generally \nabove 2500 ft. Precipitation will wind down Sunday afternoon, with \nsome sunshine possible by late in the day. Overall rainfall amounts \nwill be a quarter to a half an inch, with locally higher amounts \npossible in the higher terrain. Temperatures will return to somewhat \nseasonal normals today and especially Sunday; highs in the 50s today \nwill give way to mid 40s to mid 50s tomorrow. Winds may be a little \non the breezy side in the Champlain Valley today as north winds get \nfunneled down the valley. Strong winds then become more widespread \non Sunday, though remaining AOB 25 mph or so, with little impact \nexpected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Shortwave ridging briefly builds in behind the departing \nupper low, giving us a short respite from precipitation. However, \nanother vigorous upper trough will swing along the international \nborder Monday, with another following right in its footsteps on \nTuesday. Showers will once again develop, though remaining more \nfocused in the favored upslope areas of the northern \nAdirondacks/Greens as southwest winds shift to the west and \neventually northwest. A reinforcing shot of cold air will see snow \nlevels falling as low as 1500 ft early Tuesday morning, though the \nbulk of the precipitation will likely have ended by then, limiting \npotential snow accumulation. Total precipitation amounts will be a \nquarter of an inch or less through Monday night, with another tenth \nof two possible on Tuesday. Both days will see temperatures close to \nor just a bit above normal, generally in the mid 40s to low 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The large Hudson Bay low will continue to dominate the \nweather across our region, bringing multiple shortwaves into the \nweekend. A Shortwave pushes through on Tuesday bringing valley rain \nshowers and some chances for snow to the upper terrain locations, \nmainly above 3000 feet AGL. That snow level should drop overnight \nand give Mt. Mansfield its first chance of seeing 1\" of snow, which \nwould be the latest ever on record, breaking the record of Nov 9th \nset in 1956 and 1985. While things remain unsettled the rest of the \nweek, models are keying in on significant closed low swinging down \nthrough the Mid-Atlantic region and then up the coastline, bringing \nseveral shots of rain and rain/snow mix into the weekend. This \nsystem will definitely bare closer monitoring as the week \nprogresses.",
